Version 3.1, January 26, 2003

1/22/03  Support for IA-64 ABI

We have added support for the IA-64 ABI.  In particular, we added
the required cxxabi.h header.  See the front end Changes file
for details.

5/13/02  Missing exception specification on set_new_handler, et al

A throw specification was missing on the declarations of set_new_handler,
set_unexpected, and set_terminate.  Now fixed.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 3.0, May 9, 2002

11/1/01  exception and stdexcept headers updated

The exception and stdexcept headers did not match the specification
in the standard.  In particular, the exception class was defined in
stdexcept instead of exception.  The headers we provide are not complete
(because we don't, in general, supply library components) but to the
extent that we do provide them, they now conform to the standard.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 2.45, December 26, 2000

10/6/00  C++ header files renamed

Formerly, the C++ headers used the .h suffix.  The front end would
implicitly add the .h so that, for example, an include of <typeinfo>
would result in the inclusion of typeinfo.h.  The implicit addition of
a .h to file names with no suffix created problems when some other
file with the .h name was found in the include search path before the
desired file.

To resolve this, the files have been renamed with a new suffix that is
unlikely to be used on other header files (.stdh).  The corresponding
change has been made in the front end so that .stdh will be used when
looking for include files that have no suffix, and .h is no longer
used.

In addition, new header files with a .h suffix have been supplied for
compatibility purposes so that programs that still use names such as
new.h will continue to work.  The .h versions simply include the
version without specifying a suffix.

10/6/00  Prototype C headers removed

Formerly, the include directory contained both the C++ headers needed
to work with the EDG C++ runtime library (e.g., typeinfo) and
prototype versions of the C library header files (e.g., stdio.h).
While the C++ headers were required when using our runtime library,
the C headers, in general, must not be used because they are
incompatible with the target system's C library.  The include
directory now contains only the C++ headers.

